[] ssh-keygen to create your keys (this will prompt you for a passphrase)

[] place it under ~/.ssh/id_rsa and ~/.ssh/id_rsa.pub

[] ssh-add -l
   To list keys already installed

[] ssh-add ~/.ssh/id_rsa
   To add this key

That's it! Now provide your public key to whatever site requires it and you are in business
If you didn't have the last step, you would be asked for the passphrase each time you accessed the bitbucket repository

-------
local> ssh <username>@svn.xxx.com
remote> mkdir .ssh
remote> chmod 700 .ssh
remote> touch .ssh/authorized_keys
remote> chmod 600 .ssh/authorized_keys
remote> exit
local> cat ~/.ssh/id_rsa.pub | ssh <username>@svn.xxx.com 'cat >> .ssh/authorized_keys'


